  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'In plant growth and development studies, the C/N ratio is crucial. What is the C/N ratio? Describe its role. (3 marks)', 'The C/N ratio is the ratio of carbohydrates (carbon compounds) to nitrogenous compounds in the plant (Kraus and Kraybill, 1918). It influences the balance between vegetative and reproductive growth: when the C/N ratio is high (plenty of carbohydrates and relatively little nitrogen), plants tend to flower and set fruit; when it is low (much nitrogen), plants grow vigorously in leaves and shoots but flower poorly. Growers manipulate it, for example by limiting nitrogen, pruning roots or ringing (girdling) branches to raise the ratio and promote flowering in fruit trees. In soil, the C/N ratio of organic matter also controls how fast it decomposes and how much nitrogen is released to plants.', null, '[{"point": "C/N ratio: ratio of carbohydrates (carbon) to nitrogen (nitrogenous compounds) in the plant (or in soil organic matter)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "High C/N ratio (more carbohydrate, less nitrogen) favours flowering and fruiting (reproductive growth)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Low C/N ratio (more nitrogen) favours vegetative growth (leaves, shoots); manipulated by fertiliser, pruning, girdling; in soil it controls decomposition and N availability", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'bio-science-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
    324, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
