  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Why do X-rays and gamma rays induce mutation? (3 marks)', 'X-rays and gamma rays are high-energy, short-wavelength ionising radiations that penetrate deep into tissues. When they pass through cells they knock electrons out of atoms, producing ions and highly reactive free radicals (especially from water). These directly or indirectly damage DNA: they alter or remove bases, break one or both strands of DNA and cause chromosome breaks that lead to deletions, inversions and translocations. If the damage is not repaired correctly, the change in the DNA sequence is inherited as a mutation; that is why they are used as mutagens in mutation breeding and why exposure must be limited.', null, '[{"point": "They are high-energy ionising radiations that penetrate cells", "marks": 1}, {"point": "They ionise atoms/ form free radicals (e.g. from water), which attack DNA", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Cause base changes, single and double strand breaks and chromosomal aberrations (deletions, translocations) - mutations; used in mutation breeding", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'bio-science-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
