  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Atmospheric air contains 79% nitrogen, which is an essential plant nutrient, but most plants obtain nitrogen from the soil. Why? (3 marks)', 'Atmospheric nitrogen exists as N2 molecules held by a very strong triple bond (N≡N), so it is chemically inert and cannot be used directly by plants. Plants have no nitrogenase enzyme to break this bond; they can absorb nitrogen only in combined forms, mainly nitrate (NO3-) and ammonium (NH4+) ions, through their roots. N2 must first be ''fixed'' into these forms by nitrogen-fixing micro-organisms (Rhizobium in legume nodules, Azotobacter, cyanobacteria), by lightning or by industrial fertiliser manufacture, and organic nitrogen is converted in the soil by ammonification and nitrification. So most plants take their nitrogen from the soil.', null, '[{"point": "Atmospheric nitrogen is N2 with a very strong triple bond - inert and cannot be used directly by plants", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Plants lack nitrogenase and can absorb nitrogen only as nitrate (NO3-) or ammonium (NH4+)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "N2 must first be fixed (biological fixation by Rhizobium/ cyanobacteria, lightning, industrial fertilisers) and converted in soil (ammonification, nitrification) before roots absorb it", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'bio-science-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
